{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310701,"nonce":"misPJR25MGmU6P4SlpFFFOqEsZY=","title":"SIMD4f had FOUR bugs (found starting the nue Haxe-kernel migration). ALL FIXED: (1)+(2) operator mis-resolution + f64-\u003ef32 coercion ...","message":"Discovered while starting the nue F32-kernel-in-Haxe migration (Proof 0 = RMSNorm sum-of-squares with SIMD4f). SIMD4f is the gating primitive. FOUR bugs total; first TWO fixed in commit 2c8fb40b (SIMD4f arithmetic now correct native+wasm — add/mul/dot/splat/sqrt verified by horizontal sums in test_simd4f_values). Bugs 3-4 remain.\n\n- **Bug 3 — `.get(lane)` returned lane 0. FIXED 48c51e86.** Root: `build_simd4f_extract` (stdlib/systems.rs) hard-coded `vector_extract(self_val, 0)`, ignoring the `lane` param, so `v.get(i)`=lane0 for all i (both targets). VectorExtract needs a CONST lane, so fix extracts all 4 lanes (const) + picks runtime `lane` via a branchless **Select chain** on f32 scalars. Required adding a `Select` arm to **cranelift_backend** (normal Haxe ternaries lower to branches+phi → Select was never emitted on the native tier → W0020-trapped; LLVM/interp/c-backend already had it) + `MirBuilder::select`. Test test_simd4f_get_lane.hx (native+wasm PASS).\n- **f32 SCALAR-read gap CLOSED (no new type needed).** `SIMD4f.load(p).get(i)` reads lane i of an f32 buffer with no per-element FFI — verified `load(t.data()).get(i)` = 11,22,33,44 native+wasm. The old `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e.deref` path STILL reads garbage on f32 (Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e=F64 → 8-byte load on 4-byte data; a real Ptr\u003cFloat32\u003e/f32-deref would need a new type) but is now unnecessary — use load+get. `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e.offset(n)` byte-strided quirk still stands.\n- **SIMD4f.set(lane)** still has the pre-existing lane-0 hard-code (`build_simd4f_insert`): a vector-result select can't use the wasm UNTYPED `select` (only valid for numeric scalars, not V128); would need the wasm TYPED select. Not on the hot path; left as-is.\n\n- **Bug 4 — vector loop-phi got I64 block param → AArch64 regalloc gen_move panic. FIXED 899c073a.** `SIMD4f.load(...)` in a loop with a SIMD accumulator (`\u003c4 x f32\u003e` phi) → W0020 \"Cranelift panicked: assertion failed: to_reg.class()==from_reg.class()\". Root: cranelift_backend phi type matches (translate_phi_node_static ~2457 + phi-arg coercion ~2361) had no Vector arm, fell to `_ =\u003e I64`, so the vector phi block param was a GPR while incoming values were FPR/vector → gen_move GPR\u003c-\u003eFPR panic. Fix: add Vector arm via mir_type_to_cranelift_static to both. SIMD4f.load now compiles + runs. Test test_simd4f_loop_phi.\n- **PROOF 0 ACHIEVED (/tmp/nuechk/rms2.hx, rms1.hx):** pure-Haxe SIMD4f RMSNorm-core (sum-of-squares: load 4 f32 → fma into a SIMD acc across a loop → horizontal sum) is CORRECT native+wasm (full(0.5)→1024 both; native rand reldiff vs getFlat 5.6e-8) and **4.5× faster native / 3.6× wasm** than per-element getFlat. The orchestration + SIMD compute + buffer-load stack works end-to-end on a real kernel shape.\n- **Bug 3 (still open) — `.get(lane)` returns lane 0 for lane\u003e0.** Horizontal `sum()`/`dot()` + array-access `v[i]` are correct; explicit `.get(lane)` drops the lane index (VectorExtract path, hir_to_mir ~8974). Lower priority (kernels use sum/load/store).\n- **NEW gap — no clean f32 SCALAR read from Haxe.** `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e.deref()` on F32 tensor data returns GARBAGE (reads f64; getFlat gives right values). And `Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e.offset(n)` is BYTE-strided (offset(16)=next 4 f32), not element-strided — quirk to know. So a pure-Haxe SCALAR f32 path isn't possible via Ptr; the Proof-0 scalar baseline used getFlat (FFI). Need an f32 scalar load/store (e.g. Ptr\u003cFloat32\u003e or an f32 deref) for non-SIMD f32 Haxe code. Tensor.rand on wasm returns 0 (separate).\n\n`a + b` on SIMD4f → tast_to_hir `find_binary_operator_method` finds SIMD4f.add (@:op), can't inline (@:native, no body) → synthesizes `a.add(b)` MethodCall → mis-resolves to **`Usize_add`** (integer add), then `cast i64 to \u003c4 x f32\u003e` → emits invalid `iadd.f32` → Cranelift \"should be implemented in ISLE\" trap (W0020). The design intent (runtime_mapping.rs:3278) is operators go through the inline Binary→VectorBinOp path (hir_to_mir.rs:19186 short-circuit), but the @:op MethodCall synthesis fired first.\n**FIX (uncommitted, compip/src/ir/tast_to_hir.rs ~2137):** skip the @:op rewrite for SIMD4f Add/Sub/Mul/Div (name-guarded) so it falls through to plain binary → hir_to_mir VectorBinOp. After this: compiles + runs, no trap. But values are GARBAGE → Bug 2.\n\nHaxe `Float` is f64; SIMD4f lanes are f32. `SIMD4f.splat(1.0)` lowers to `const 1f64; call SIMD4f_splat($f64)` with NO demote → the f64 bit-pattern lands in f32 lanes. Proof: `splat(4.0)` then `sqrt` → `[0,1.5,0,1.5]` (4.0_f64 bits = 0x4010..00 split into f32 lanes ≈ 2.25/0); `a+b=[0,0,0,0]`; `dot=0`. Deterministic on BOTH Cranelift and LLVM tiers.\nThe demote HELPER exists: `maybe_box_for_extern_call` (hir_to_mir.rs:24421) handles `(F64,F32)=\u003ebuild_cast` (fdemote, which cranelift_backend:2421 supports). And `get_stdlib_mir_wrapper_signature(\"SIMD4f_splat\")` returns `[F32]` (registered in runtime_mapping register_simd4f_methods). So the general mir-wrapper arg loop (hir_to_mir.rs:12939) WOULD coerce — but the SIMD4f STATIC-method call (splat/make/load/fromArray) takes a different lowering path (the SIMD4f direct-lookup, ast_lowering.rs:12624-12642) that bypasses the maybe_box coercion. **NEXT: find the SIMD4f static-call MIR lowering and route its float args through maybe_box_for_extern_call / insert the F64→F32 demote against the [F32] declared param types.** Instance ops (sum/dot/get) return f64 and may need the reverse promote on extract.\n\n- compiler/examples/test_simd_e2e.rs — SIMD4f testbed. **WEAKNESS: it only asserts \"Execution succeeded\", NOT output VALUES — so it reports 17/17 PASS on garbage.** Strengthen it to assert expected lane values before trusting it (cf [[feedback_regression_output_coherence]]).\n- compiler/examples/test_tensor_e2e.rs — Tensor/QTensor testbed, to use when converting them to @:coreType abstracts.\n\nadd/sub/mul/div are declared BOTH `@:op(A+B)` and as `@:native` methods. The\nOPERATOR form (`a + b`) lowered to VectorBinOp (correct), but the explicit\nMETHOD-CALL form (`a.add(b)`) routed through the SIMD4f MIR-wrapper path, which\nmishandled the vector ABI: `a.add(b).sum()` returned ~1.7e38 garbage, and in a\nloop-carried accumulator it trapped W0020 \"Unsupported cast I64 (Ptr(Void)) -\u003e\nVector F32x4\" (the SIMD value carried as a generic pointer). This is why the\n\"add/mul verified correct\" claim above held — test_simd4f_values used OPERATORS,\nnever the explicit method form. FIX: intercept SIMD4f add/sub/mul/div method\ncalls in the method-call lowering (hir_to_mir ~12926) and emit VectorBinOp\ndirectly, mirroring the operator path (~19541). Restricted to rayzor_SIMD4f\n(f32x4); i32x4 excluded (integer VectorBinOp miscompiles on wasm, see\n[[project_simd4i_decision]]). dot/sum/sqrt/abs/min/max/get already worked via\ntheir wrappers — only the 4 arithmetic ops were affected. Test\ntest_simd4f_methods.hx (methods==operators + loop accumulator). Found via the\nuser's \"can't afford flaky simd4f\" while pursuing the SIMD-inlining lever (B2).\nSEPARATELY re-confirmed [[perf_haxe_qmatmul_blocked_on_simd_inlining]]'s \"wrong\ndiagnosis #1\": extending the post-merge InliningPass to the native JIT path\n(compile_helpers gate wasm→wasm||jit) gave NO measurable speedup (dot bench\n0.20ms both, warm) — native Cranelift self-inlines small leaves — so that gate\nextension was REVERTED; the wasm-only gate is correct.\n\nPer [[project_workerpool_fix_chain]] the parallelism layer is done. The migration plan's first proof point (move an F32 kernel like RMSNorm to Haxe = SIMD4f + Ptr + WorkerPool) is BLOCKED on SIMD4f producing correct values. Fix both bugs (operator + f64/f32) then redo Proof 0 (scalar-vs-SIMD sum-of-squares bench, /tmp/nuechk/rms0.hx). The integer-SIMD dot primitive for the quant matmul (the hottest path) is a separate, later gap. User direction: Tensor/QTensor become @:coreType abstracts (zero method-call overhead); flash-attention fusion stays FFI. UPDATE 2026-06-29: that abstract conversion was tried + is a VERIFIED behavioral NO-OP (kernels stay @:native FFI; abstract changes type modeling not the call) — kept extern class. See [[project_tensor_coretype_abstract_noop]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_simd4f_broken_two_part` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}